FILED s543p (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-27, CODE 0ab08642 = MAIN-READY-111, v2-16300) by the Rosetta census (docs/rosetta-plan.md step 1; #2104).

THE SHAPE (neutral reproducer, run on the tree, perl 5.40.3 vs PCL):

    my @v = (4, 5, 6);
    my @C = (0);
    $C[0] += @v[1];            print "a=@C\n";     # perl a=5    PCL a=1
    my %h = (k => 0);
    $h{k} += @v[2];            print "b=$h{k}\n";  # perl b=6    PCL b=1
    my $r = [4, 5, 6];
    $C[0] = 0;
    $C[0] += $r->@[1];         print "c=@C\n";     # perl c=5    PCL c=1   (the postfix slice, the program's spelling)
    my $s = 0;
    $s += @v[1];               print "d=$s\n";     # perl d=5    PCL d=5   (a scalar target is right)
    $C[0] = 100;
    $C[0] -= @v[0, 1];         print "e=@C\n";     # perl e=95   PCL e=98  (count 2, not the last element 5)
    $C[0] = "";
    $C[0] .= @v[1];            print "f=@C\n";     # perl f=5    PCL f=5   (.= is right)

WHERE (emitted Lisp): the scalar target is `(p-incf-raw $s (p-list-scalar (p-aslice @v 1)))` -- the slice's last element; the element target is `(p-incf (p-aref @C 0) (p-aslice @v 1))` -- the raw slice vector, which numifies as its length.  The numeric compound-assignment lowering for an element lvalue does not impose scalar context on its right operand (the scalar-target path does; `.=` does).

CHEAP DISCRIMINATING MEASUREMENT (taken): the d=/a= pair above -- the same right side, a scalar target right and an element target wrong, so it is the element arm of the compound-assignment lowering, not the slice.  A one-element slice in scalar context is common in programs written as `@a[$i]` (a classic beginner spelling) and in postfix `->@[$i]`.

THE PROGRAM: 0190-Centroid-of-a-set-of-N-dimensional-point sums coordinates into an array element with `+=` from a postfix one-element slice; every sum counts 1 per point.


=== DONE (s543v) === (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-28; WIP 2 adbdfeb9 on base 38420fae, generation v2-16800)
THE MEASUREMENT (perl 5.40.3): the right operand of EVERY `OP=` -- `+=` `-=` `*=` `/=` `%=` `**=` `<<=` `>>=` `&=` `|=` `^=` `x=` `.=` `||=` `&&=` `//=` -- is SCALAR context for every target (an element, a deref element `$r->[0]` / `$$r[0]`, `$h{a}{b}`, `$_[0]`, a tied element, `our`, `substr(...) x=`): a slice is its last element, an array its count, a call sees wantarray false, a comma list its last element.  The base was right only for a raw-slot scalar target (Parser2 lowers that right side on its own, already scalar) and for `.=`; an element / boxed / `our` target inherited the statement's void / list context (`$C[0] += @v[1]` added the COUNT; `$C[0] += f()` / `$h{x} ||= f()` called f in VOID context).  `(@a) x= 2` is a perl compile error.
THE FIX (Pl/PExpr.pm child_context, the one place every operator's operands get their context): child 1 of `Pl::PExpr::TokenUtils::is_compound_assign` is SCALAR_CTX.  ir-spec §4.1.
PROBES perl -> base -> tree: scratch/s543v/probes/m2-measure.pl 53 rows (base 22, tree 53, PCL_OPT=none 53), ctx.pl 8 (base 6).  The hot paths `$a[$i]++` / `$h{$k} += 1` / `$x += $y` are byte-identical in emission.  Guard Pl/t/elem-assign-lvalue-01.t row 6, FAILS on 38420fae.  Rosetta 0190 `same`.
